clueweb09-en0003-18-21513.60 x 9 in. 240 pages, with color photos and drawings Published 1999 ISBN 978-1-56158-292-1 Product #070424 Who can resist the allure of a white picket fence? In this visually stunning guide, George Nash celebrates the art of the wooden fence in its full range of styles, with practical information on designing, building, and maintaining a fence. Color photographs by veteran National Geographic photographer James P. Blair make this book as beautiful as it is informative.
clueweb09-en0003-18-21513.120 National Geographic photographer James P. Blair make this book as beautiful as it is informative. An experienced carpenter and talented writer, Nash traces the evolution of the American wooden fence from Colonial times to the present. He discusses the various types of fences, and explains how to design a fence to meet every conceivable need -- including how to be a good neighbor. From laying out the fence to digging postholes, anchoring the posts, and
clueweb09-en0003-18-21513.180 a good neighbor. From laying out the fence to digging postholes, anchoring the posts, and installing gates and fence boards, Nash provides a variety of practical and useful information. There's even a troubleshooting guide for maintenance and repair. Introduction The Evolution of the American Fence Wooden Fence Types Design Factors Fence Materials Planning & Layout Building a Fence Gates Maintenance & Repair Index When my wife and I bought our quaintly down-at-the-heels mid-19th century Cape
